    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Serer religion, also called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serer">Serer</a> spirituality, is known to its followers as A ƭat Roog, which means "the way of the Divine", "path of God" or "religious life". It is the original set of religious beliefs, practices and teachings of the Serer people of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegambia">Senegambia</a> region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>. The ethnologist Simone Kalis writes that it is based on the cult of the ancestors, the pangool, around which all the rituals of religious and therapeutic practice turn. The priest, the o yaal pangool, stands halfway between the world of the beyond and the world here below, between the invisible and the visible, the dead and the living.</p><p>Its history is tied to the founding of Serer villages: a group of pangool is said to have accompanied each village founder, called a lamane, the ancient kings, as he travelled in search of land, and the lamane then created shrines to these pangool and became their priests and custodians. The Raan festival is held at the old village of Tukar, founded by Lamane Jegan Joof around the 11th century after he migrated from Lambaye, north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-sine">Sine</a>, guided by Saint Luguuñ Joof.</p><p>The Serer people are held to have originated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal-river">Senegal River</a> valley, at the border of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a>, and to have moved south in the 11th and 12th centuries and again in the 15th and 16th. The scholar Dennis Galvan writes that the Serer peoples migrated south from the Fuuta Tooro region beginning around the eleventh century, entering the Siin and Saluum river valleys; the economist Étienne Van de Walle gives a slightly later date, placing the formation of the Serer ethnic group in the thirteenth century. In the late 14th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandinka">Mandinka</a> migrants led by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guelowar">Gelwaar</a> clan entered the area of Sine and met Serers who had already established a system of lamanic authorities.</p><p>The Senegambian stone circles, which were probably built by the Serer, were also a place of worship: laterite megaliths were carved, planted and directed towards the sky.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="beliefs">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Beliefs &amp; Practice</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serer">Serers</a> believe in a universal supreme deity, Roog or Rog, sometimes called Roog Sene, "Roog The Immensity" or "The Merciful God", and seen as the embodiment of the male and female. Offerings are made to Roog at the foot of trees such as the baobab, at the sea and rivers, and in people's homes or community shrines. Roog is not the devil nor a genie but the "Lord of creation"; Roog sees, knows and hears everything but does not interfere in the day-to-day affairs of the living, and lesser gods and goddesses act as Roog's assistants. Among them are the fangool Mendiss, a female protector of the Fatick Region, the god Tiurakh, god of wealth, and the god Takhar, god of justice or vengeance.</p><p>Ordinary Serers address their prayers to the pangool, the ancestral spirits and saints, who are held to be intermediaries between the living world and the Divine, and prayers are accompanied by ancient songs and sacrifices of a bull, sheep, goat, chicken or harvested crops. The immortality of the soul and reincarnation, ciiɗ, are strongly held beliefs. The righteous, accepted by their ancestors, may enter Jaaniiw, the place where good souls go, and reincarnate; one rejected becomes a lost and wandering soul. Serer spirituality rejects the notion of heaven and hell, and of hell fire. Each family has a totem, the tiim, both a prohibition and a guardian.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="texts">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Texts &amp; Teachers</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Serer religion has no written scripture. Its traditional practices include ancient chants and poems, the veneration of and offerings to the pangool, initiation rites, folk medicine, divination, and the preservation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serer">Serer</a> history, culture and identity. The priest, the o yaal pangool, is the master of the cult, and the Serer creation myth belongs to the same oral tradition. Chants are sung when prayers are addressed to the pangool, and Serers also pass on their history and identity through these songs and poems.</p><p>The Serer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/griots">griots</a> have a religious role at the death of a king. On the death of a Serer king, the Fara Lamb <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-sine">Sine</a>, the chief griot of the Kingdom of Sine, buries the king's treasured drum, the junjung, with him; his other drums are played for the last time before being buried in the ground facing east, and the griots chant ancient songs of sadness and praise. The last time this ceremony was held was on 8 March 1969, after the death of the last king of Sine, Maad a Sinig Mahecor Joof.</p><p>The Serer cosmos has its symbols. The star Yoonir, which is Sirius, the brightest star in the night sky, is sacred because it announces the beginning of flooding and lets farmers begin to plant. The Star of Yoonir is also drawn as a sign: its top point stands for Roog and the other four for the cardinal points, and Serers who cannot read or write the Latin alphabet often sign official documents with it.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="spread">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Spread</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Serer religion belongs to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serer">Serer</a> people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegambia">Senegambia</a>, and its later history is one of encounters with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. Accounts of Serer resistance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamisation">Islamisation</a> reach back to the 11th century and the Almoravid movement. By around 1030 to 1035, under the Muslim leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-jabi">War Jabi</a>, the Serers refused forced conversion and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">Sharia</a> law; they were defeated, and the first Serer exodus from Tekrur followed, south to where they mixed with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jola">Diola</a>. This exodus affected only the Serers living in Tekrur at the time, not all Serer people.</p><p>In the 19th century the Serers fought several prominent African Islamic jihadists. According to the historian James Olson, they "violently resisted the expansion of Islam" by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolof">Wolof</a>, and they were a target of the 1861 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> led by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandinka">Mandinka</a> cleric Ma Ba Jaxoo. Maba Diakhou Bâ, considered a national hero and given saint-like status by Senegambian Muslims, was killed in battle against the Serer king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-sine">Sine</a>, Kumba Ndoffene Famak Joof, on 18 July 1867 at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-fandane-thiouthioune">Battle of Fandane-Thiouthioune</a>, commonly called the Battle of Somb. At the surprise attacks on Naodorou, Kaymor and Ngaye, where the Serers were defeated, many villagers killed themselves rather than be conquered by the Muslim forces, among them some who jumped to their deaths at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">Well</a> of Tahompa. In Serer religion suicide is permitted only if it satisfies the principle of Jom, honour.</p><p>The inter-ethnic wars involving the Serer continued until 1887, when French colonial forces conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a>, and conversion then accelerated. By the early 1910s about 40% of the Serer people had adopted Islam, and by the 1990s about 85% were Muslims. Most Serers converted to Islam and to Roman Catholic Christianity only after colonisation; they and the Jola were the last in the region to convert. Many still follow the Serer religion, especially in the ancient Kingdom of Sine, although Senegal and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-gambia">Gambia</a> are predominantly Muslim countries. Their long resistance to Islamisation has created a division between "believers" of Islam and "non-believers" such as the orthodox Serers who adhere to the Serer religion. Some Serers syncretise, mixing Islam or Christianity with the old religion.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="community">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Community &amp; Society</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serer">Serer</a> are an ethnoreligious group, and the religion is bound up with preserving Serer identity. Endogamy is strongly adhered to: the Serer religion forbids interfaith, interethnic and interracial marriage, and banishment and disinheritance are among the punishments for marrying out. The Serer-Noon, a subgroup, are especially strict about it. Marriage alliances are formed between maternal and paternal clans, and the tokoor, the head of a matriclan, usually its oldest male, tends to arrange marriages. A woman must not be engaged in a physical relationship until marriage, and a couple found in a premarital relationship were both exiled. Adultery is dealt with by the law of compensation, Mbaax Dak A Tiit, and in the past a family could forgive or avenge a killing at a gathering before the chief or king.</p><p>Religious life has its own rhythm. Monday is the day of rest and, with Thursday, a day of offerings and libations to the pangool, and Thursday is also a day on which weddings and Senegalese wrestling are prohibited. The Mbeel is the law of charity and hospitality, and the Jom is a code of religious, social, economic, political and ecological values. Men and women may be initiated into the secret order of the Saltigue, which is reserved for a small number of insiders. The dead from the upper ranks of society were mummified and buried in pyramid-shaped tombs with grave goods; mummification is less common now.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Serer religion survives in the practices of its followers and in the ceremonies that gather them. The Xooy ceremony takes place at Fatick, usually beginning in the first week of June, when the Saltigue, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serer">Serer</a> high priests and priestesses, foretell the future in front of the community, including the coming weather, politics and economy. It brings thousands of people to Holy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-sine">Sine</a> from all over the world, among them Serers who follow only the old religion, Serers who syncretise, and non-Serers such as the Lebou. The Raan festival is held at Tukar every year on the second Thursday after the new moon in April, and some villages of Sine hold an annual rain ceremony, A Cuur Ale.</p><p>Serer knowledge of herbalism, passed down over years of learning, is taught at a school and centre set up by the Senegalese government; the CEMETRA, the experimental centre of traditional medicine at Fatick, has at least 550 professional Serer healers as members in Sine-Saloum.</p><p>The religion is also held to have left its mark on other faiths of the region. The Serers are one of very few communities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegambia">Senegambia</a>, apart from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jola">Jola</a>, whose name for god is not borrowed from Arabic. Senegambian Muslims are said to have taken the names of old Serer festivals, among them Tobaski, Gamo, Korite and Weri Kor, to name Islamic festivals in their own language.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Serer religion, A ƭat Roog, is the original religion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serer">Serer</a> people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegambia">Senegambia</a>, built on the supreme deity Roog, the veneration of ancestral spirits called pangool, family totems and the divination of the Saltigue priests. The Serers resisted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamisation">Islamisation</a> from the 11th century to the 19th; most converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> after colonisation, but many in the old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-sine">Kingdom of Sine</a> still follow it.</em></p>

